<?php
/** User manual — Staff edition: what every Golden A/S employee does for himself in the portal — leave, program, training, evaluations, community.   GAS-CP-R63-003 */
return [
  'title' => 'Customer Portal — Employee Guide',
  'lede'  => 'Your own corner of the portal: your leave balance and requests, the leave periods you can join, your program on the Planning board, the trainings you can apply for and your certificates, your annual evaluation, your messages with HR, and the Community — wishes, kudos, the noticeboard and badges. Short, with the steps to follow.',
  'sections' => [
    ['id' => 'start', 'title' => 'Getting in and getting around', 'body' => [
      'Parts to invoice — delivery notes to accounting (v1.138.0): every line of an issued delivery note to a project or a customer now passes through invoicing, like the man-hours. Open 🧾 Parts to invoice from Procurement › Delivery notes or from Accounting. Four tabs: To decide, To be invoiced, Included in the offer, Invoiced. (1) Procurement (the Procurement right or management) works on "To decide": the lines are grouped per vessel with delivery note, project, part, quantity and unit cost; the unit price is proposed at cost plus the minimum markup and can be changed; choose per line "To be invoiced" or "Included in the offer" — or set all lines at once with the buttons on top — and press Save decisions. Lines left on "—" stay to be decided. The accounting department gets a notification. (2) Accounting works on "To be invoiced": tick the lines that are on one invoice, type the invoice number and date and press "Mark ticked as invoiced"; "Back to procurement" returns lines that should not be invoiced as they are. (3) "Included in the offer" needs no invoice; procurement can still change the decision. (4) "Invoiced" shows number, date and who registered it; for a wrong entry accounting ticks the lines and presses "Undo the invoice", then registers them again. Delivery notes to production (subcontractors) and returns to suppliers are not part of this. The page ⚓ To invoice per vessel shows the same lines of the vessel under "Parts on delivery notes". Note: all lines of delivery notes issued before this version start under "To decide".',
      'Delivery time on offers (v1.137.0): the offer form (Commercial › Offers › an offer › Edit, offers made with the offer builder) has two fields below Validity — "Estimated delivery time — parts" and "Estimated delivery time — labour". Type them as the customer should read them, e.g. "6–8 weeks from order" and "2 weeks after the parts arrive". The PDF and the Word document print a numbered section "Delivery time" with the two lines, after Exclusions and before Validity; a field left empty is not printed, and with both empty there is no such section. If your text templates already have their own delivery wording, put {delivery_parts} and {delivery_labour} in that text block instead — the document then prints your block and no separate section. A firm offer created from a budgetary one keeps the two values.',
      'To invoice per vessel (v1.136.0): on Accounting, click a vessel in "Waiting to be reported" — or the button ⚓ To invoice per vessel and choose one — to see everything that is still to be invoiced for that vessel, not only the hours. Five figures on top and a table for each: (1) Contracts not fully invoiced — every sub-project (or project) with an approved offer, its contract value, what was invoiced and what remains; a red "Final invoice" marks a sub-project or project that is completed, so that invoice closes it; the Invoice button opens a direct invoice for it. (2) Proformas without an accounting invoice — issued proformas not yet covered by an invoice; highlighted when money was already received. (3) Hours not reported yet — approved hours of timesheets and service reports per project and sub-project, with Collect to start the accounting report. (4) Accounting reports not invoiced yet — drafts, returned reports and those waiting at accounting. (5) Materials of service reports — parts marked "I" (to be invoiced) on service reports that are not invoiced yet; 📦 = issued from stock. Amounts are gross as in the ledger and are shown only to people with the finance view or the accounting department. Materials bought through purchase orders for a project are not listed — they are invoiced through the offer of the project.',
      'Correcting an invoice number or date afterwards (v1.135.1): when accounting typed a wrong invoice number or date on an accounting report, open the report — below the detail there is "✎ Correct the invoice number or date" (accounting department and administrators only). It lists every invoice registered on the report with its number, its date and the number of lines; change the number and / or the date, add a reason if you wish, and press Save correction. Only the number and the date change — the lines stay Invoiced, hours and values are untouched; the timesheets show the new number, and the History of the report records "Invoice corrected" with old → new, who and when. An invoice that was issued from the portal (direct invoice) keeps its own number in the ledger — correct that one on the invoice itself.',
      'Two ERP codes per organisation (v1.135.0): the ERP numbers customers and suppliers in two separate ledgers, so an organisation has two fields — "ERP code — customer" and "ERP code — supplier" (Contacts › Companies › edit). The customer code is the one of the billing customer (Settings › Customers shows the same); the supplier code is the one of the supplier card (Procurement › Suppliers shows the same) and is saved only when the organisation has the role Supplier. The two are never copied into each other any more, the supplier import fills only the supplier code, and the same number may exist in both ledgers for different organisations. A supplier code can belong to one organisation only. Contacts › Organisations shows "ERP codes to tidy" while there is something to fix: suppliers that carry their code in the customer field (one button moves them all) and organisations that are customer and supplier with the same code on both sides (open each and type the right codes from the ERP).',
      'Sub-projects of the chosen project only (v1.134.1): on every form with a Project and a Sub-project field — new task, ticket, service and inspection report, function check, offer, production order, direct invoice, requisition, purchase order, planning entry, accounting and external service reports — choosing the project narrows the Sub-project list to the sub-projects of that project. The New task form and the Planning form used to show every sub-project in the search list; this is corrected. On the task form, with no project chosen, the list is grouped under a heading per project, and choosing a sub-project fills in its project. If the list still shows everything after the update, reload the page once (Ctrl+F5).',
      'Profit below the minimum — the red ▼ (v1.134.0): a System Budget compares its profit with the company minimum, 40 % on cost unless management changed it ("Markup on cost": sale = cost × 1,40; this is 28,6 % margin on sale). On the budget page the Totals card shows a red notice when the whole budget is below the minimum, and the Markup on cost of Labour, Parts and the Total turns red with a ▼. Every labour line and part line whose own profit is below the minimum — usually because of a line discount or a lower percentage typed on the line — carries a ▼ next to its sale; point at it to read the figures. When the total is fine but some lines are not, a grey notice says how many. Travelling expenses and other costs are not marked (they are normally passed on at cost), and excluded parts are skipped. In the list of budgets a ▼ stands next to the sale of a budget below the minimum. The preview before "Convert to offer" shows the Markup on cost of the offer in red with a notice when it is below the minimum (it counts the commission when the offer includes it). The mark is a warning only — nothing is blocked. Administrators and management set the percentage at the top of Work › System Budgets ("Minimum profit on cost %"; 0 switches the mark off).',
      'Apps showing "Sync error" (v1.133.3): between 10 and 12 November 2026 the phone apps (Service Engineer, Golden PM, Golden HR and the customer app) could not reach the portal and showed "Sync error". This is repaired on the server — no app update is needed. Open the app and pull to refresh, or wait for the next automatic sync: reports, hours, photos and requests that were saved on the phone in the meantime are still in its queue and are sent now. If an app still shows the error, sign out and in again.',
      'Project buttons with all sub-projects completed (v1.133.2): on Work › Projects a project whose sub-projects are all completed shows "n completed hidden · Show completed" and, below it, the usual buttons — Edit project, Add sub-project, Drawings, Weekly and Monthly report, Offers, Job Orders, Service Reports and (administrators) Delete. Before, the buttons appeared only after "Show completed".',
      'System Budgets grouped per vessel (v1.133.1): Work › System Budgets lists the budgets under a heading per vessel — the vessel of the budget\'s project — with the number of budgets, the hours and the sale of that vessel; the vessel name opens the vessel page. The vessels follow the order of their most urgent budget, so the vessel with the most pressing request is on top, and inside a vessel the budgets keep the order priority › date the offer must be ready. Budgets that have no project yet come last under "Without a vessel". The filter "Group by" switches between Vessel and No grouping (the flat list, which can be sorted by any column), and the search box also finds a vessel name.',
      'Web Sales (v1.133.0): the Website inbox and the Website statistics moved from Work to Commercial › Web Sales — one menu entry, one page with three tabs: Website inbox (the number beside it is the count of new inquiries), Website statistics and Marine intelligence. Links in notifications open the right tab as before.',
      'Marine intelligence (v1.133.0): Commercial › Web Sales › Marine intelligence shows the headlines of the week in five groups — Newbuilding yachts (Europe and Middle East), Refit news (Europe), Energy storage systems for marine, Hybrid vessels (marine technology) and Yachting technology news. Each line gives the headline, the source and the date; a click opens the article on the publisher\'s site in a new tab. "Older week" and "Newer week" move through the last six months. The portal reads its news sources by itself, one at a time through the day; "Read the sources now" reads them all at once. The grouping is done by the words of the headline, so now and then a headline lands in the wrong group or is not relevant: management and sales can Move it to another group or Hide it, and can add a headline by hand (title, link, group, short note).',
      'Yard, flag and class in the vessel list (v1.132.1): Work › Vessels shows Yard, Flag and Class as three columns (a dash where the field is empty), and the search box also finds a flag or a class — type "Malta" or "RINA" to list those vessels.',
      'Class and flag of a vessel (v1.132.0): the vessel form has the fields Class (classification society — a list suggests the usual ones, any text is accepted) and Flag, beside IMO and Yard. They show under the vessel name on its page, under the yard in the vessel list, and in the Excel export.',
      'E-mail card — draft in your Outlook (v1.131.1): the button "Draft in my Outlook" places the message in the Drafts folder of your own Microsoft 365 mailbox — in HTML format, with the recipients, Cc, subject, text and the ticked attachments (up to 25 MB). Open Outlook › Drafts — the new Outlook, Outlook on the web and the phone all show it within a few seconds — change what you like and press Send; it is sent from your address like any message you write yourself. The portal also shows a button that opens the draft in Outlook on the web. Use it when you want to read or edit the message before it leaves; use "Send now" when it can go as it is. The file "Draft file for classic Outlook (.eml)" stays for the classic Outlook only. Administrators: this button needs the application permission Mail.ReadWrite (with admin consent) on the portal mailer in Microsoft Entra; without it the portal answers that Microsoft 365 did not allow the draft.',
      'E-mail card — send from your own mailbox (v1.131.0): the ✉ E-mail card of an offer, a report, a proforma, a purchase order, a statement or a candidate has three buttons. "Send now from my mailbox" (gold) sends the message at once through Microsoft 365 from your own address, in HTML format with the ticked attachments; it is kept in the Sent Items of your Outlook and replies come to you — nothing has to be opened or downloaded. The portal asks for confirmation first, because the message leaves immediately; check recipients, subject and text before. Limits: attachments up to 2,9 MB together, 30 recipients, 40 messages per hour — above that use the draft. "Outlook draft (.eml)" downloads the message for classic Outlook, now in HTML format, to edit before sending (the new Outlook and Outlook on the web cannot edit such a file). "Mail app" opens the default mail program with recipients and text only. "Send now" appears when the portal sends through Microsoft Graph and your portal e-mail address is a company mailbox; administrators switch it on or off, and see every message sent this way, in Settings › System › Website.',
      'Markup on cost and margin on sale in a System Budget (v1.130.1): the percentage typed for parts — in the header ("Markup on parts") and on a part line ("Markup on cost %") — is added to the cost: sale = cost + that percentage of the cost. The Totals card shows the profit of each group in two columns: "Markup on cost" = (sale − cost) ÷ cost, the figure you typed, and "Margin on sale" = (sale − cost) ÷ sale. They are two readings of the same profit: 50 % on cost is 33,3 % on sale, 40 % on cost is 28,6 % on sale; to keep half of the sale price as profit the markup on cost must be 100 %. Only the names changed — no price of any budget changed.',
      'System Budget from a sub-project (v1.130.0): open the sub-project — the card "System Budgets" (under the offers) lists the budgets of that sub-project with number, priority, status, the date the offer must be ready, owner, who asked and the sale. To start the process fill only two fields: Priority and "Offer ready by" (optional, not in the past), then "Request a System Budget". The budget is created under the sub-project and its project, as a draft, with the title [PROJECT]-[SUB-PROJECT]-[Description] (for example GY-85-02-Y.01-Bridge); nothing else has to be typed. It appears at once in Work › System Budgets, where the list is ordered by priority and an overdue date shows in red — the technical department works from there. The project manager of the project gets a notification. The person who asks is the first owner; the owner can be changed in the budget header. If the sub-project already has an open budget the portal asks before creating another. Shown to the people who may use System Budgets (management, project managers, sales).',
      'Travelling expenses at cost plus a percentage (v1.129.0): the table "Travelling expenses and other costs" of a System Budget has the column "On cost %". Type the cost and a percentage on a line — the Sale becomes cost + that percentage (10 on a cost of 400,00 gives 440,00; 0 means at cost) and a sale typed on the same line is replaced. Leave the percentage empty to type the sale yourself, as before. The percentage is kept in revisions, copies, templates and the Excel export.',
      'Discount per line in a System Budget (v1.129.0): the Labour table and the Parts table have the column "Disc. %". Type a percentage (0–100) on a line and save: the Sale of that line is lowered by it — for a labour line the whole line, hours and daily allowance; for a part line quantity × unit sale. The cost does not change, so the margin of the budget shows what the discount costs. The undiscounted amount stays visible, struck through, under the sale; the total row of each table and the Totals card show the sum of "Line discounts given". An empty field is no discount. The discount is kept in revisions, copies, templates and the Excel export. At the conversion to an offer with "one line per line" each offer line carries its discount %, so the customer sees price, discount and net amount; the daily allowance appears as one line per discount percentage. With grouped labour (per section, one lump sum) or parts as a lump sum the offer line carries the already discounted amount and shows no percentage.',
      'Rate cards per zone in a System Budget (v1.128.3): the Rate card list of a labour line shows the hourly rate of the budget\'s zone — with the zone Outside Attica / Abroad / Greek islands it shows and applies the out-of-Attica rate of the rate list (before, a rate list with separate rows for the two zones gave the local rate for both). A budget that was priced with the wrong rate shows the yellow note on the labour card; "Apply the rate-card rates" corrects its lines.',
      'Rate zone of a System Budget (v1.128.2): the header field "Rate zone" has two values, the two price columns of the rate list — Local (Attica) and Outside Attica / Abroad / Greek islands (one column covers all three). Changing the zone and saving the header now re-prices the labour lines that carry a rate card: a line that had the card\'s rate of the old zone takes the rate of the new zone, and the totals, the offer preview and the conversion follow. A rate that was typed by hand (or came from an imported workbook) is kept; the labour card then shows a yellow note with the number of such lines and the button "Apply the rate-card rates", which sets every rate-card line to the rate of the zone. Lines without a rate card (flat-rate or free lines) are not touched — give them a rate card or change the rate by hand. A finished budget must be reopened first.',
      'Preview of a vessel\'s web link (v1.128.1): when a vessel has a web link, its page shows a preview card under the name — the site, the title of the linked page and its short description (usually the vessel\'s particulars) — with the button "Open the page", which opens the page in a new tab. In Work › Vessels a 🌐 icon beside the vessel name opens the same page directly. Nobody has to open Edit to reach the link. The portal reads the title and the description when the link is saved; ↻ on the card reads them again. A page that cannot be read (it needs a sign-in, or it blocks automatic readers) shows the site name and the button only. Shown to Golden A/S staff only.',
      'Web link of a vessel (v1.128.0): Work › Vessels › Edit has the field "Online information (web link)" — the address of a place where information about the vessel is kept online (a shared folder, the yard\'s or the manager\'s page, a register entry). The vessel\'s page then shows the button "Online information" with the name of the site; it opens in a new tab. Only an address starting with http:// or https:// is accepted (www… is completed with https://). The button is shown to Golden A/S staff only — customers do not see it.',
      'Signed out when you come back (v1.127.7): the portal signs a person out when its last tab has been closed for more than about 45 seconds ("the browser was closed" — Settings › Login history › Sign out when the browser is closed; management can switch it off). Opening the portal after that leads straight to the sign-in page with that message — the dashboard no longer appears for a moment first. Going from the portal to the entry page goldensas.com and back in the same browser keeps you signed in while that page stays open.',
      'Who is online: the green counter on the topbar opens the list of colleagues connected now with their time online; My Account › Who is online shows the sign-in time, the uptime and the last activity. Sign in with your company e-mail and password; the code of the authenticator app follows (two-factor sign-in). The first time: My Account › set your photo (it appears next to your name everywhere), your language (English, Greek, Italian, Bulgarian, Ukrainian), the theme (light / dark) and your world clocks — up to seven places for the clock strip at the top of every page (UTC is always first; Back to the company set restores the default). Passkeys (My Account › Passkeys): add this device once — your phone or PC asks for Face ID / fingerprint / PIN — and from then on the login page offers "Sign in with a passkey" (your browser may suggest it in the e-mail field), replacing password and authenticator code; a passkey is one device (or your Apple / Google keychain) bound to your account, nothing biometric leaves the device; remove it on the same page if the device is lost. The same passkey signs you in to the mobile apps (login screen › Sign in with Face ID / passkey) when the app runs in the browser or installed from the browser. 🔔 Notifications at the top is where every decision, wish and reminder arrives.',
      'The left menu — what matters for you: Dashboard (your attention list, celebrations, fleet & activity, your program of the week), HR › My leave (balances, requests, periods, trainings, evaluations, messages with HR), Work › Planning (where you are planned, day by day), Community 🤝, Support › Manual and Knowledge base. Accounts scoped "Employee" see only My program, My leave, the manual, notifications and the account. On a phone the same My leave page is in the Golden A/S Service app under More › My HR (install from goldensas.com/customer/mobile/).',
      'Leaves allowed by law (v1.86.5): My leave, the Service app\'s My HR and the HR app no longer list parental, paternity / maternity, marriage, carer, force majeure, child illness, study, school hours, sick and L.104 leave among the entitlements. They sit in their own block "Allowed by law — on request" with the ceiling the law provides ("up to 5", "per event") and what was taken — never an "available" figure — with a note that they are not an entitlement to be used up but what the law allows when the event occurs, on request and with the supporting document. Annual leave, paid hours and early-leave hours remain entitlements.',
      'Reason and medical certificate (v1.87.0): every leave request needs a reason chosen from a list that follows the leave type (vacation, personal, family, bereavement, wedding, exams, public duty, force majeure … for leave; illness, accident, medical examination, surgery, hospitalisation for sick; birth, child care, child illness, school, prenatal for parental); "Other" needs a description. The manager may correct the reason when approving (the decision row has the reason selector) and HR may correct it at any time (✎ Correct reason under the request). A request for a medical reason may be sent without the doctor\'s certificate: it gets a due date 5 working days after the first day of absence, shown as a gold "certificate due by" pill (red when overdue) with an Upload button — on My leave, in the Service app and for HR under the request. The employee is reminded the day before, employee and HR when it is overdue; the HR dashboard lists the certificates still due. Works the same in the Service app (0.6.15) and the Golden HR app (1.0.3).',
      'Missing supporting documents (v1.87.1): My leave opens with a notice listing the requests that still lack their document — a medical certificate with its due date (red when overdue) or a type that requires a document filed without one — each with its upload button below; the Service app shows the same notice on My HR and a red badge on the home card; on the HR side the person\'s card carries a "📄 n supporting document(s) missing" pill on the Requests tab and the HR dashboard lists the medical certificates due. Leave reasons are translated in all portal languages (EN, EL, IT, BG, UK) on the web and in the apps.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'balance', 'title' => 'My leave — balances', 'body' => [
      'HR › My leave shows for the year, per leave type: entitled (the statutory annual leave is computed by the portal from your service — 20 to 26 days, pro rata in the year of hiring), carried over from last year, taken, pending (requested, not yet decided) and available; red means over the entitlement. Hour types accumulate in hours: school visits have their own allowance (4 days per child a year, in hours); early leaves / late arrivals are a company permission — the hours add up and every completed 8 hours takes one day of your annual leave; what is left is shown under your annual balance ("4 h accumulated · 4 h to the next day"). If a figure looks wrong, write to HR from the same page (Messages with HR) — HR can see the computation and correct it.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'request', 'title' => 'Requesting leave', 'body' => [
      ['ol' => ['My leave › New request: choose the type (annual leave, sick, paternity / maternity, marriage, child care, school visit, strike, unpaid …), the dates (or hours for hour types) and the reason when the type asks for it; attach the document when the type requires one (medical certificate …). The portal counts working days only — weekends and public holidays are never charged.', 'Send. Your manager decides first (types marked HR only, such as sick leave, go straight to HR), then HR. You are notified at every step; a note from the approver appears with the decision.', 'Approved leave appears on the Planning board (one chip per day) and moves from pending to taken; a rejected request returns the days to your balance.', 'While a request is pending you can cancel it yourself; afterwards ask HR to correct it.']],
      ['fig' => 'flow-timesheet.svg', 'cap' => 'The approval ladder — you, your manager, HR, then the board'],
      'Maternity and paternity leave are once per birth: HR keeps your children\'s birth years on your card; days already taken at a previous employer for the same birth are deducted — tell HR.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'periods', 'title' => 'Leave periods, groups and company closures', 'body' => [
      'HR publishes leave periods (the summer groups, a quiet week in a yard) on My leave › Open periods. Join one with your own dates inside the period — your manager approves and the leave is recorded without a second request. Periods marked 🔒 Mandatory are company closures (Christmas week, August): HR assigns them to everybody, you cannot join or opt out; the working days of the closure are charged to your annual leave automatically. "Full" means the places of a period are taken.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'program', 'title' => 'My program — the Planning board', 'body' => [
      'System Budgets (v1.123.0): the Work › System Budgets menu is shown to Sales, project managers and management only. If you need to see a budget, ask your project manager.',
      'Engineer on service-report time lines (v1.116.1): write the engineer who actually did the hours on every line — accounting bills by that name. The first line is pre-filled with your own name only if you are technical staff; when you prepare a report for colleagues the field starts empty and the list offers the technical staff.',
      'Specialty on service-report time lines (v1.114.1): pick the technical discipline the hours belong to — Electrical, Automation, Navigation & Communication, Service, Engineering, Production, Project Management — each shown with its rate card (e.g. "Automation · card D — Automation engineer") — plus the specialist cards of the rate list (Atlas engineers, Magnus, Aventics …) for hours billed at those tariffs; it is pre-filled with your own discipline. Accounting bills those hours at that discipline\'s tariff, so choose the discipline of the work actually done, not your job title.',
      'Completed sub-projects (v1.114.0): a sub-project the PM set to Completed no longer appears in the sub-project lists of your forms (timesheets, service and inspection reports, tasks, tickets, drawings, risks) nor in the Projects tree — the tree says "n completed hidden ›" to show them. A report or timesheet that already points to a completed sub-project keeps it.',
      'Work › Planning (or My program for employee accounts) shows where you are planned day by day: sub-projects and vessels in colour, your leave, the days off of your calendar. Click a chip to see the sub-project, the open items and the contacts. The week view on the dashboard shows the next seven days. Changes are made by the project managers and HR — if your program is wrong, tell your PM.',
      ['fig' => 'g-colour-code.jpg', 'cap' => 'The colour code of the chips'],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'training', 'title' => 'Training opportunities, my trainings and certificates', 'body' => [
      ['ol' => ['My leave › Training opportunities lists the trainings you are invited to — introductory, internal or external — with dates, hours, places and the apply-by date. Open the programme, add a note if you like and press Apply. Your manager or HR approves; you are notified.', 'My trainings keeps your record: status (applied, approved, attended), result and certificate — number, valid-until date and the scanned copy to download. Expiring certificates are followed by HR; the refresher is published as a new training.', 'Introductory trainings are part of the onboarding of a new colleague; internal ones are given by Golden A/S engineers — propose topics to HR through Messages with HR.']],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'evaluation', 'title' => 'My annual evaluation', 'body' => [
      'My leave › How we evaluate you explains the whole method and shows your computed scores so far for the running year — read it at the start of the year, not at the end. Once a year your manager (with HR) writes your evaluation: eight criteria scored 1 to 5 — quality of work, productivity, technical skills, teamwork, initiative, reliability, safety & procedures, communication — with strengths, areas to improve, goals for next year and training needs; two more scores are computed by the portal from the year\'s facts — training participation (what you attended, ranked among colleagues) and attendance & consistency (early leaves / late arrivals, unplanned absences, leave asked at the last moment) — and the targets of the year are summed up. When it is final you are notified; read it on My leave › My evaluations, add your own comment if you wish, and press Acknowledge. Acknowledging means you have read it, not necessarily that you agree — write your view in the comment; it stays with the evaluation. Your evaluations of past years stay on the same page.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'utilization', 'title' => 'Your utilization (v1.98.0)', 'body' => [
      'HR and your manager see, per month and per year, how your working time was used: hours on projects from your timesheets, internal work and absences from the planning board, and the rest as idle hours — days without a project or work. A day you worked but did not book on a timesheet counts as idle, so book every day on the week\'s timesheet and submit it; planned days without a timesheet are flagged. Your own report is under HR › Utilization when the page is open to you.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'targets', 'title' => 'My targets', 'body' => [
      'My leave › My targets lists the targets of the year that concern you — the company\'s, your department\'s and your own — with how each is measured, the target value, the due date, the progress and the status. Update the progress of your own targets whenever something moves (a note helps your manager); your manager closes them as achieved or missed at year end. New targets reach you as a notification. Their achievement is part of your annual evaluation.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'messages', 'title' => 'Messages with HR and your personal data', 'body' => [
      'My leave › Messages with HR is a private thread between you and HR — nobody else, not even your manager, can read it. Use it for balances, certificates, documents, personal data changes (address, phone, bank account, family status — HR keeps them on your card; you can see your own data on My leave). HR is notified of every message and you of every reply.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'community', 'title' => 'Community — wishes, kudos, noticeboard, badges', 'body' => [
      'Celebrations on the dashboard: the birthdays, name days, work anniversaries and new colleagues of the next 14 days. On the day (and the day before) a Send wishes button appears — pick an emoji, add a few words, send; the colleague is notified and sees everybody\'s wishes on his own dashboard. Every morning the portal posts the celebrations of the day in Notifications.',
      'Kudos: thank a colleague for a job well done — from the dashboard or Community, choose the person and say what for in one line (up to three a day to the same person). The kudos wall and the most-thanked lists are on Community.',
      'Noticeboard (Community): company news, polls and photos. React (👍 ❤️ 👏 🎉 😂 🚢), comment — type @ to mention a colleague, who is notified — vote; share a photo of the month from the field, or attach a photo to any post or poll (jpg / png / webp up to 12 MB). Badges are earned automatically from your work in the portal (timesheets, tickets done, reports, Q&A answers, trainings attended, kudos …) and show next to your name. The winner of the month is named from the activity ranking of the dashboard — the score counts what you create in the portal: tickets, comments, timesheets, reports, answers, requests.',
      'New colleague: your onboarding checklist and your buddy are on your dashboard until all eight items are done — photo, manual, personal data checked, first timesheet, found on the Planning board, leave balance checked, introductory training, coffee with the buddy.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'card', 'title' => 'Completing my employee card with the HR Assistant', 'body' => [
      'My leave shows how complete your employee card is. "Complete my card with the HR Assistant" opens a guided flow in six short steps: identity and family, contact and address, identification numbers and bank, education and work, documents, review and submit. Every field is pre-filled with what HR holds today — confirm, correct or leave it. You can stop at any time and continue later; the same flow is reachable from HR › HR Assistant.',
      ['ul' => [
        'Nothing is written to your card before HR approves. Your identification numbers (AMKA, ΑΦΜ, ID, passport, IBAN) are encrypted while the flow is open and only HR can read them.',
        'The first step shows how your data is handled (who is responsible, why it is kept, who sees it, how long, your rights); tick "I have read the notice" to begin.',
        'The portal checks what you type: the AMKA must be 11 digits starting with a date (normally your date of birth — if yours was issued with another date, tick "My AMKA was issued with this date" and HR will see the note), the ΑΦΜ check digit and the IBAN must be valid. If your account has two-factor authentication, the identification step and the submission ask for the 6-digit code.',
        '✦ Let the assistant fill it in: describe your details in your own words, or photograph your ID card or passport. The assistant proposes values — press Use on the ones that are right; the photo is only read, not stored. Attach the scan itself in the Documents step (PDF, JPG, PNG, WebP).',
        'Review & submit lists only what you changed, next to what the card holds; tick the declaration and submit. HR may return the flow with a note (you will find it on top of the flow), reject it, or approve it — you are notified each time, and the approved values appear on your card.',
        'Withdraw cancels an open flow; the proposed values are discarded.',
      ]],
    ]],
    ['id' => 'requisitions', 'title' => 'Asking for parts — purchase requisitions (v1.91.0)', 'body' => [
      'If your discipline is enabled for requisitions (or you are a PM), the left menu shows Procurement › Requisitions. "+ New requisition": pick the project and sub-project, the purpose (project, stock, service call) and the date you need the parts by; create it, then add the lines — search the inventory by code or description (the picker shows the free stock) or type a free-text line with maker and part number. "Submit to Procurement" freezes the lines; Procurement checks the parts budget of the sub-project and approves (stock reserved, the rest bought), sends it to management if it is over budget, or rejects with a reason — you are notified at every step and can follow the log on the request. The warehouse issues the reserved parts to you; the request closes when every line is issued.',
      'Engineering BOM (v1.92.0): if you are in the team of a sub-project (or your discipline is enabled), the Procurement tab of the sub-project lets you keep its bill of materials as revisions — upload the file or type the lines, see what the stock covers, issue the revision to Procurement, and answer their change proposals (accept or reject with a reason). The approved revision is the current BOM; one click makes a requisition for the lines the stock does not cover.',
      'BOM import (v1.113.0): use the template from the BOM page; after the upload review each row — the portal proposes the inventory item (by Golden code, maker part number or the assistant) with its stock; Accept, Reject (free text), pick another item or create a New inventory item, then Apply.',
      'Goods receipt (v1.94.0, Warehouse flag): on a sent purchase order the Goods receipt box lets you book what arrived per line — received and rejected quantities with the reason (damaged, wrong type, missing) and the action (return, replacement, credit), delivery note and a scan. Accepted parts go into the warehouse at the order price and are reserved for the requisition they were bought for; the requester is told and you issue them from the requisition as usual.',
      'Item documents and software licences (v1.105.0): on an inventory item card you find the links Procurement added — datasheet, manual, drawing, certificate, software download, supplier page. Software items show their licences (product, version, seats, expiry, where assigned); the licence key itself is visible only to procurement administrators. When a delivery note was issued to your project, the goods are already out of the warehouse — the note (number, PDF) is linked on the purchase order receipt and listed under Inventory › Delivery notes.',
      'Parts from the phone (v1.97.0 — Service app 0.6.18, Golden HR 1.0.4; v1.103.1: tap the item box to see the recently used items): Home › 🧾 Parts & requisitions lists your requisitions (Open / Mine / All) and "+ New requisition" raises one from the phone — project, sub-project, purpose, needed-by, title, then lines: search the inventory by code or description, scan the barcode with 📷, or type a free-text part with maker and part number, set the quantity and unit, "+ Add line", and Submit to Procurement. Open a request to follow it: state, every line with reserved · ordered · received · issued, the log of decisions, and Cancel while nothing has been reserved. You need to be online; the same request appears in the browser under Procurement › Requisitions.',
      'Hours not chargeable (v1.119.0): when hours on your service report must not be charged to the customer, set the line to NC and choose why — Warranty, Goodwill, Internal / own work, Training, Rework or Other (portal form and Service app 0.6.21). You decide it once, on the report; accounting only confirms it.',
      'Parts on service reports (v1.96.0): next to each spare part of a task type the inventory code (or scan the barcode with the 📷 button in the Service app 0.6.17) and the quantity; when you save the report the part is taken off the stock automatically and costed to the job, and a 📦 mark appears. Parts that are not in the inventory stay as plain text.',
    ]],
    ['id' => 'help', 'title' => 'Help and good habits', 'body' => [
      ['ul' => ['Support › Knowledge base: ask a question, answer a colleague\'s — answers earn a badge.', 'Support › Portal requests: report a bug or propose an improvement; the developer answers with a decision and the release that carries it.', 'Keep your leave requests ahead of time — approved leave is what the PMs plan around.', 'Check Notifications once a day; the Monday digest sums up the week (celebrations, open trainings, new posts, last week\'s top three).', 'Your password and two-factor device are yours alone — never share them; sign out on shared computers.']],
